f 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1061 17469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1061 17471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1061 17477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1061 17478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1061 17885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1061 17886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1061 17887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1061 17888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1061 18457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1061 18458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1061 23264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1065 3193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1064 "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1065 3194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1065 3245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121048 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1065 3316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121055 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129132 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128943 #edb/ref 17592186045417]